{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 460 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "44f8ec7a6a2269d5c42c4cd1df5819852d8f7afbf32c3d567cbebb7911e76bfa" :edb/db-before-t 459 :edb/db-after-t 460 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533772 50 #inst "2026-10-04T22:04:18.738Z" 13194139533772 true] [17592186064097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186250154 13194139533772 true] [17592186064195 1020 #edb/ref 17592186250151 13194139533772 true] [17592186096764 1020 #edb/ref 17592186250163 13194139533772 true] [17592186115103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186250160 13194139533772 true] [17592186119691 1020 #edb/ref 17592186250157 13194139533772 true] [17592186124591 1020 #edb/ref 17592186250148 13194139533772 true] [17592186184146 1020 #edb/ref 17592186250145 13194139533772 true] [17592186250145 1017 #edb/ref 17592186250146 13194139533772 true] [17592186250145 1089 "q-167566" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250145 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533772 true] [17592186250145 1100 "The velocity, in $\\text{m/s}$, of a subatomic particle is modeled using the function $v(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t)$ for $\\pi <t<2\\pi .$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250145 1113 #uuid "93039f81-d254-59b7-bb23-afc67d93c39b" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250145 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13942110/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n2{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=2{(-2)}^{2}=8.\n$$" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250146 1000 #edb/ref 17592186250147 13194139533772 true] [17592186250146 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533772 true] [17592186250146 1041 "field-1" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250146 1104 #edb/ref 17592186250147 13194139533772 true] [17592186250146 1110 #uuid "814efb60-5471-5ee7-a6c4-d64b4126f57d" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250147 1034 "8" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250147 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533772 true] [17592186250147 1111 #uuid "4243ff74-259e-5230-becf-285644e45f80" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250148 1017 #edb/ref 17592186250149 13194139533772 true] [17592186250148 1089 "q-167567" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250148 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533772 true] [17592186250148 1100 "The velocity, in $\\text{m/s}$, of a subatomic particle is modeled using the function $v(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t)$ for $\\pi <t<2\\pi .$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $8$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250148 1113 #uuid "61ff54f9-7eef-527f-ad64-ca38683902ed" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250148 1145 "Substituting $t=2\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{csc}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =2\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13942110/assets/361e8bf3b622fc2e73be712dae4ec5d189bd15eecbd33c9295db7365bcf71943.png)\n\nAs $t$ approaches $2\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t$ are negative and decrease without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=-\\infty \n$$\n\nApplying the product and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi ) & ={lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}{csc}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the positive constant $2$ does not change the sign of the limit, so we have\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {({lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t))}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250149 1000 #edb/ref 17592186250150 13194139533772 true] [17592186250149 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533772 true] [17592186250149 1041 "field-1" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250149 1104 #edb/ref 17592186250150 13194139533772 true] [17592186250149 1110 #uuid "4ac2db53-238c-5be1-8039-5b40526f1777" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250150 1034 "\\infty" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250150 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533772 true] [17592186250150 1111 #uuid "842e4736-6732-5d54-8ee1-7b1f768523d6" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250151 1017 #edb/ref 17592186250152 13194139533772 true] [17592186250151 1089 "q-167568" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250151 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533772 true] [17592186250151 1100 "The velocity, in $\\text{m/s}$, of a subatomic particle is modeled using the function $v(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t)$ for $\\pi <t<2\\pi .$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                