                                     $-4\\csc(t)^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡t$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n$\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2\\cot(t)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\text{ }C$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nAnother particle has a velocity function given by ${v}_{2}(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Evaluate the velocity of this particle at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250157 1113 #uuid "c74073fa-e61f-5b08-b5be-fb67408d3773" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250157 1145 "Evaluating the function ${v}_{2}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})=(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nFirst, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13942110/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})=(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250158 1000 #edb/ref 17592186250159 13194139533772 true] [17592186250158 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533772 true] [17592186250158 1041 "field-1" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250158 1104 #edb/ref 17592186250159 13194139533772 true] [17592186250158 1110 #uuid "329508af-47f0-5285-ba36-769738acc228" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250159 1034 "-\\frac{8\\pi}{3}" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250159 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533772 true] [17592186250159 1111 #uuid "538265ee-1061-5103-982e-a2941124ad24" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250160 1017 #edb/ref 17592186250161 13194139533772 true] [17592186250160 1089 "q-167571" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250160 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533772 true] [17592186250160