       \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡t$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n$\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2\\cot(t)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\text{ }C$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nAnother particle has a velocity function given by ${v}_{2}(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Evaluate the velocity of this particle at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{8\\pi}{3}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nFind ${lim}_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}{v}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{5\\pi\\sqrt{2}}{4}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the acceleration function ${a}_{2}(t)$ of the second particle.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${a}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250163 1113 #uuid "d736a850-66a6-5820-ac9d-0fa81a1d204a" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250163 1145 "In part 3, we saw that the derivative of a particle's velocity with respect to time gives its acceleration.\n\nTo differentiate ${v}_{2}(t),$ we require the product rule. Let $g(t)=t$ and $h(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Then\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=1,\\,{h}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe used the chain rule to calculate ${h}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2}(t) & ={v}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)+g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))+(t)(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186250165 13194139533772 true] [17592186250164 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533772 true] [17592186250164 1041 "field-1" 13194139533772 true] [17592186250164 1104 